        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "refactor(release): simplify monthly extended-stable plugin snapshots",
        "body": "Depends on #100111.\n\nThis is a stacked draft. PR #100111's head (`b313d30c991aa1cd4fdf1f478bb7e61295aee0e0`) is the base of the implementation branch, but that branch exists only in the contributor fork and therefore cannot be selected as an upstream base branch. Review the post-#100111 range `b313d30c991aa1cd4fdf1f478bb7e61295aee0e0...ed9b67af3c67f29ec1f3b773552ba370b5e1495b`. After #100111 lands, this branch should be rebased onto `main` before the PR is marked ready.\n\n## What Problem This Solves\n\nExtended-stable core releases need a deterministic plugin compatibility boundary. Without one, uncovered official plugins can follow the moving npm `latest` line independently of the monthly extended-stable core, while covered plugins can miss the exact core patch.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThis introduces one monthly snapshot rule:\n\n- At `YYYY.M.33`, publish and promote core plus every npm-publishable official plugin.\n- At `YYYY.M.34+`, publish and promote core plus the covered plugins: Slack, Discord, and Codex.\n- For an extended-stable core on that monthly line, uncovered official plugins with default/latest intent resolve to exact `YYYY.M.33`; covered plugins resolve to the exact core version.\n\nThe implementation does not maintain a generated cohort file. Exact pins, disabled or denied plugins, third-party npm packages, ClawHub, Git, path, and archive installs remain unchanged. Existing `stable -> latest` behavior is unchanged.\n\nThe release handoff carries one authoritative publication set plus snapshot readbacks. Production activation remains gated on a protected `openclaw/releases` selector consumer that supports handoff schema v2 and `promote_extended_stable`.\n\n## User Impact\n\nThe first extended-stable release each month creates a complete official plugin snapshot. Subsequent extended-stable patches advance core and the covered plugins while other official plugins remain on that month's `.33` snapshot.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Current head: `ed9b67af3c67f29ec1f3b773552ba370b5e1495b`.\n- Focused runtime verification: 7 files / 467 tests passed.\n- Focused release verification: 13 files / 250 tests passed.\n- Handoff-schema verification: 4 files / 15 tests passed.\n- Current-head main CI in the fork passed 100/100 jobs, with no unresolved review threads or merge conflicts.\n- Static inventory proves 69 official npm plugin packages: 3 covered plus 66 monthly-snapshot packages, with runtime/release membership parity.\n- An ephemeral Verdaccio canary passed on the exact current head:\n  - `.33`: core, Slack, Discord, Codex, and uncovered Matrix were published and installed through `extended-stable`.\n  - `.34`: core and the three covered plugins advanced; Matrix remained exactly `.33`; Matrix `.34` was absent.\n  - Fresh CLI install and update resolved core and covered plugins to `.34` and Matrix to `.33`.\n  - Core tarball SHA-256: `.33` `4b28777bbbf8396c488d8496803f8abafd0049a11311becf8802b05ec82dea80`; `.34` `caddf6d6aa9c160b88a4c069c913c288fa2537afc8d63f810084e15b1fb72d6b`.\n  - Candidate tags and the ephemeral registry were cleaned after the run.\n\nThe local canary used a private package identity and registry. It did not exercise public npm publishing, GitHub environment approval, OIDC/provenance, root declaration generation, or the external protected selector consumer.\n",
        "branch": "dev/kevinlin/extended-stable-plugin-cohort",
